WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.629 --> 00:00:02.145
- Originally owned by the daughter

00:00:02.145 --> 00:00:04.197
of Marie Antoinette's governess,

00:00:04.197 --> 00:00:08.364
this château is a trove of
hidden architectural gems.

00:00:12.928 --> 00:00:17.234
About 30 miles outside of Paris
stands Château de Groussay.

00:00:17.234 --> 00:00:21.912
Built in 1815, it remained a
family home until the 1930s

00:00:21.912 --> 00:00:24.496
when it was bought by
an Italian arts patron

00:00:24.496 --> 00:00:27.852
who made modifications including
Chinese inspired gardens,

00:00:27.852 --> 00:00:30.352
the Tartar Tent and a theater.

00:00:31.372 --> 00:00:34.213
The private mini theater
of Château de Groussay

00:00:34.213 --> 00:00:35.463
opened in 1957,

00:00:36.372 --> 00:00:40.347
and is inspired by a baroque
opera house in Germany.

00:00:40.347 --> 00:00:42.717
Today it is still active
with plays and concerts

00:00:42.717 --> 00:00:46.219
taking place throughout the year.

00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:49.636
One of the châteaus most
admired features is the library.

00:00:49.636 --> 00:00:52.296
It is said that it was
Cecil Beaton's inspiration

00:00:52.296 --> 00:00:55.575
for the library in the film My Fair Lady,

00:00:55.575 --> 00:00:59.026
but the most unusual feature
of all is the Tartar Tent,

00:00:59.026 --> 00:01:00.844
a painted metal pavilion,

00:01:00.844 --> 00:01:02.783
but made to look like a fabric tent;

00:01:02.783 --> 00:01:07.602
the interior of which
features 10,000 delft tiles.

00:01:07.602 --> 00:01:10.980
The château was declared a
historic monument in 1993,

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.639
and it's open to the
public for weekend visits,

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:15.139
or special events.

00:01:16.311 --> 00:01:17.762
A walk through this château

00:01:17.762 --> 00:01:20.929
really shows how the other half lived.

